Record what an agent does to a directory, without launching it.

Starts an effects-only session for the directory (default: the current one): every file created, edited, renamed or deleted there is recorded, by whatever process makes the change — a GUI agent, an IDE, or an agent session that is already running. There is no wrapped command, so there is no terminal output or transcript. Runs until Ctrl-C, `hh watch --stop`, or --idle-timeout. One watcher per directory: a second `hh watch` on the same directory reports the running session instead of starting another. Refuses your home directory and filesystem roots.

Usage: hh watch [OPTIONS] [PATH]

Arguments:
  [PATH]
          Directory to record. Defaults to the current directory

Options:
      --daemon
          Detach and keep watching in the background (writes a PID file under the data dir). Stop it with `hh watch --stop`. Not available on Windows yet

      --status
          Show running watchers and their sessions (only the one for PATH, if given)

      --stop
          Stop the watcher for the directory and finalize its session. Not available on Windows yet

      --idle-timeout <DURATION>
          Finish the session after this long with no file activity, e.g. `90s`, `15m`, `2h` (a bare number is seconds). Off by default

      --name <NAME>
          Name the recorded session (shown by `hh inspect`; change it later with `hh rename`)

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Examples:
  hh watch                       record changes to the current directory until Ctrl-C
  hh watch ~/code/site --daemon  watch in the background
  hh watch --status              list running watchers
  hh watch ~/code/site --stop    stop it and finalize the session
  hh watch --idle-timeout 15m    finish after 15 minutes without changes

Docs: https://halfhand.org/docs/watch/
